SEC-4 phased — data-plane token authentication, live end to end
2026-08-10 · timelakedb-da (plaintext, TIMELAKE_DATA_AUTH=required)
================================================================================

THE CLAIM: one token, issued from the console, authenticates a request
on BOTH data-plane doors (HTTP line protocol + /api/sql, and Flight SQL
over the tonic accept loop), under the three header spellings stock
clients send. In `required` mode a request without a valid token is
refused on every path. The mechanism was fixed by measurement first —
see data-auth-client-probe.log — not chosen.

Run in a real container, because the in-process router tests cannot
exercise the Flight SQL accept loop where the gRPC guard actually runs.

REQUIRED MODE, NO CREDENTIAL — every door locked
  POST /api/v3/write_lp        401
  POST /api/sql                401
  Flight SQL GetFlightInfo     REFUSED "authentication required" (Unauthenticated)

WITH A CONSOLE-ISSUED read_write TOKEN
  issued via POST /admin/tokens (admin session), secret shown once,
  prefix tldb_
  POST /api/v3/write_lp  Bearer   204
  POST /api/sql          Bearer   [{"n":2}]
  POST /api/sql          Token    200   (Telegraf influxdb_v2 spelling)
  Flight SQL             Bearer   OK rows=1  (through the accept loop)

SCOPE SEPARATES SHIPPING FROM READING
  A write-only token attempting a Flight SQL read:
    REFUSED "token is not permitted to do that" (permission_denied / 403)
  This is the property that lets Tributary hold a credential that can
  ship but cannot read the database back — the reason Scope is not a
  total order.

THE SPLIT IS MEASURED (valid Prometheus, column-0)
  timelake_data_auth_mode 2                              (0=off 1=optional 2=required)
  timelake_data_requests_authenticated_total 1
  timelake_data_requests_anonymous_total     0
  timelake_data_requests_rejected_total       1

  Same lesson as want-mode mTLS: an operator flips optional -> required
  on this measured split, not on a guess. In `required` the anonymous
  counter must stay 0; a non-zero value means something is still
  connecting without a credential and the flip was premature.

WHY THE THREE SPELLINGS
  Bearer  — Grafana (Flight SQL token field), Tributary
  Token   — Telegraf influxdb_v2 output
  Basic   — Telegraf influxdb v1 output (token as the password)
  All three carry ONE token on the Authorization header. This is also
  what InfluxDB accepts, so the compatibility matrix keeps its shape.

MODES AS A MIGRATION, NOT A SWITCH
  off       the data plane does not read Authorization at all — today's
            documented contract, so a Telegraf carrying a leftover
            InfluxDB token keeps writing unmodified. Verified: a garbage
            token in off mode is ignored, not refused.
  optional  anonymous still served; a PRESENTED-but-invalid token is
            refused (fail loud on day one, not on flip day); a valid one
            is identified and its grants applied.
  required  no valid token, no access — both doors.

ENFORCEMENT IS ONE DECISION, NOT TWO
  HTTP and Flight SQL both call Engine::authenticate_data_impl ->
  Auth::decide_data -> guard::decide. A token that cannot read over
  Flight cannot read over /api/sql, because there is one implementation
  of the rule. Flight re-authenticates at DoGet as well as at planning,
  because a ticket is client-crafted bytes — planning-time auth alone
  would be a door with no lock on the second entrance.

SECRETS
  256-bit OS-CSPRNG secret, stored only as a SHA-256 digest (not
  Argon2id — a token has no brute-force surface to slow, and a ~50 ms
  KDF on the write path would be a self-inflicted RR-1 violation).
  Shown once at issue; revocation is a tombstone and takes effect on the
  next request, verified live.

WORKSPACE
  116 tests (auth 20, +7 server data_auth integration, flight/api wired),
  clippy clean, fmt clean.

NOT DELIVERED HERE (named, not left looking done)
